gs said which you'll probably  regret but  yeah and just remember like everyone  just into it like they remember a  penalty early on the game and everyone  was just like coming in like  trying to fight and stuff everyone was  so in the red and the crowd was so  massive and on top of us that  there's just so much energy and i think  everyone's emotions and that whole game  got the better of them but  it came out on the right side yeah that  was pretty cool you were captain  for that as well yeah yeah that's pretty  awesome  do you when you're in those moment  because like the emotion thing that's  pretty important thing to touch on as  well because  when you're running hot like that you  don't necessarily get the best out of  yourself yeah  and i feel like i've probably haven't  played as well as i could have in finals  before and i you see  some players who like you said are just  so consistent  in those key big moments and they've  just got their emotions so under control  and  you see it in league with guys like cam  smith jonathan  where they're just they don't ever look  they're just on one level  exactly and they like not it doesn't  look like they're angry or  ever too emotional about it and  just looks like another game or every  everyday game where  my mindset definitely wasn't there like  i was playing in the final in front of  30 000 people just like so wired up  right  i took it yeah i think i took a fair bit  of learning out of that just to  try and keep it as another game it is  just enough  but um there's just a bit more writing  on it  what's your perspective on you're moving  into sort of another phase or  setting up for another phase of your  life now where potentially you might  play a bit more rugby but you  obviously we've covered and with your  injury and everything you're sort of  looking at the next  next phase what does that look like for  you you've talked about coaching and  stuff like that do you  do you have ambitions to be in coaching  i've always felt like coaching sort of  been my calling more than  playing to be honest and um  i if i didn't have a family and a wife i  would 100  be going down the coaching dream chasing  that but the thing with the coaching  is that you're still living that rugby  player lifestyle where you're moving  around and i mean we have  as a family we haven't stayed in the  same place longer than a year and a half  and that was when we moved to london  and every six months other than that  we've been moving around so we've moved  to italy  now centering wellington taranaki and  things like that  japan wellington japan wellington so as  a family  my voice starts school next year we're  so keen to just settle down somewhere  and find a base so  if it wasn't for that i'd love to be a  coach but  um i just i'm not going to chase the  coaching dream by moving around if a  coaching job opportunity came up  where i was and i'd 100 percent jump at  that and would love to do that but  um if anyone from tesla marcus is  listening  sign them up yeah i guess  values change over time and exactly and  it's  the priority now i've had my time living  my dream and  doing my things so i feel like now's the  time to  let the kids settle down and live a good  life and  stop moving them around because it does  get pretty hard on them yeah nice  you're a good man jimmy i appreciate it  chatting man and just want to wish you  all the best for your rehab  uh and for whatever's next i personally  hope that you get back out on the field  never run around i've always enjoyed  supporting you and watching you play  um i know that having known you from  when we're at high school that rugby was  always the dream so it's pretty awesome  to chat to you  uh and that sort of full circle thing  and it's just  yeah i hope that the next phase goes  really well for you bro yeah cheers mate  thanks for having me on and hopefully we  can have sort of the 19 combo next time  that was probably the chubbiest number  nine you've ever seen  cheers mate so there you have it guys  that was the interview with james  marshall  that was a really cool conversation for  me because james and i were friends at  high school  and we played a lot of rugby together in  college and it was always his dream to  go out there and make it as a  professional rugby player so  to sort of get the opportunity to sit  down with him and have that conversation  10 years down the track and reflect on  his  journey from college right through to  playing professional rugby here in new  zealand and also abroad  it's just a really cool conversation  i've got a lot of respect for anyone who  goes out there and chases their dream  and there's a lot of valuable insights  in there for any young guy in particular  who wants to go out there and make it in  professional sports so  i hope you found value in that if you  did please share it out with anyone that  you think would enjoy listening to it  uh and feel free to follow james on  instagram it's at james marshall  he's actually just launched his own  podcast called the what a lad podcast  which is james sitting down and having  conversations with some of the best lads  that he's met through his playing days  and talking about all things  professional sport and also diving into  some of the antics they get up to off  the field so  give that one a listen and check that  one out like i said share that out with  anyone that you think would get value  from it  and i look forward to connecting with  you in the next episode  [Music]  you
 

